<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      Declared HERE rather than in app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml on purpose: the permission
      exists because of this module and disappears with it, so a future "offline flavour" that
      drops :feature-pairing drops the permission with it and needs no manifest edit.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
      INTERNET, for pairing with a computer and for nothing else.

      This file used to say INTERNET was deliberately absent, because "pairing is a screen-to-camera
      exchange between two phones in the same room; it opens no socket". That sentence is still true
      of pairing with a PHONE, and that flow is unchanged: two phones exchange two QR codes and this
      module opens nothing.

      What changed is that a laptop has no camera to point at the second QR code. So when (and only
      when) the scanned QR1 names a rendezvous server, this module POSTs the SEALED bundle there
      and the computer collects it. Concretely:

      1. Nothing here contacts anything until the user has scanned a computer's pairing code AND
         pressed "Send" on a screen that names the host it is about to send to. The address is
         unauthenticated (it arrives in the clear in a QR code), which is exactly why it is shown
         and confirmed rather than acted on.

      2. Exactly one request is made, to exactly that host: one POST of one opaque string. There is
         no discovery, no fallback address, no bundled default, no update check, and redirects are
         refused so a response cannot move the request to a different host.

      3. What is sent is AES-256-GCM ciphertext under a key derived from an ECDH whose private
         halves never left either device. The server stores it, cannot open it, and deletes it when
         the computer collects it. See core-pairing's RendezvousProtocol for exactly what the server
         does and does not learn.

      4. There is deliberately no analytics, crash reporting or telemetry dependency in this module,
         and adding one would change what this permission means.

      The manifest merger unions this with the identical declaration in :core-sync-net, so the merged
      application manifest is unchanged. It is declared here as well rather than leaned on from
      there, because a build that dropped :core-sync-net would otherwise silently break this module.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      Both pairing roles have to scan (device A scans QR1, device B scans QR2), so the feature
      genuinely needs a camera. `required="false"` keeps the app installable on a camera-less
      device (an emulator image, some tablets) where every other screen still works; the
      runtime check in the scanner reports the absence instead of crashing.
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.camera.any"
        android:required="false" />

</manifest>
